# Target C all-defect phase/train gap

Date: 2026-09-12

Lifecycle: audited-working proof-only consequence; theorem-state authority unchanged.
C-II and Level I remain open.

For the connected nonisolated positive-contact core, count every contact edge in the
complete stopped-train decomposition, including singleton trains on edges incident
to no strict rhombus. Let `T` be the train count, `q` the loaded-hull vertex count,
`f` the bounded nonquadrilateral-face count, `d_H=q-h`, and
`s_Z=e+h-(2v-2)`. The exact train/core ledger is

```
T-q = 2f-(d_H+s_Z).                              (1)
```

On a Type-II full-core survivor sequence, near-disk convergence and vanishing
strict-rhombus area excess force the normalized crossing measure onto the three
projective phase relations. Simplicity of the stopped-train crossing graph and
weighted Mantel give

```
2 sqrt(Delta_2) <= liminf (T-q/2)/D,
Delta_2 = pi/(2 sqrt(3)).
```

The exposed hull intervals give the matching upper relation between loaded vertices
and the atomic train mass. Since `q/D <= pi`, the combined inequality yields

```
liminf (T-q)/D >= 2 sqrt(Delta_2)-pi/2
                   = 0.333829286933018... .       (2)
```

Combining (1) and (2), every Type-II survivor has

```
liminf [2f-(d_H+s_Z)]/D >= 0.333829286933018...,
```

or equivalently

```
f >= (d_H+s_Z)/2 + 0.166914643466509...D-o(D).
```

The proof is robust to holes, bridges, articulation points, exceptional faces of
arbitrary degree, and contact-isolated source points because the affine-kernel and
train ledgers are applied only to the nonisolated core. It does not infer a global
minimizer from a local phase limit, nor does it close the inactive-container branch.

The result is useful as a face-population lower bound. It is equivalent to the
already-known strict phase gap after the exact train identity, so a further closing
step must charge these forced faces to a source-preserving descent, a response bound,
or a sharper global route payment.
